Not quite.  The word "her", like "my", "your", "our" and "their" is 
strictly termed a possessive adjective, while the words "hers", "mine", 
"yours", "ours" and "theirs" are possessive pronouns.  The first group 
have to be adjectives, because they always modify a noun while the last 
are pronouns because they are used in place of a noun.  Now, just as 
"walk" can be a noun or a verb ("I went for a walk" vs "I will walk 
later"), so "his" can be either a possessive adjective or a possessive 
pronoun ("I have his war-hammer" vs "The spellbook is his"), probably 
because possessive pronouns are mostly possessive adjectives with an "s" 
tacked onto the end, and "his" already ends in an "s".


